"One of the interesting features I built is an AI Inventory Investigation Agent for our inventory management system.

The main problem we wanted to solve was that whenever a warehouse manager asked questions like:

Why is this GR not issued?
Why is this item out of stock?
Why is allocation still pending?

they had to manually open multiple modules like Inventory, Material Movement, Allocation, Receiving, and Picking. It usually took around 30 to 60 minutes to find the actual reason.

So we built an AI agent that can answer these questions automatically in a few seconds.

How it works

The user simply types a question in normal English.

For example,

"Why is GR 1234 still not issued?"

This request first comes to our Inventory Management microservice, where the AI Agent is implemented.

Inside the service, we create an investigation context with a unique investigation ID and send the user's question to Google Gemini.

Along with the question, we also provide Gemini with a list of available tools. These tools are actually backend APIs that can fetch real business data.

For example, we have tools like:

Get Current Stock
Get Inventory Movements
Get Allocation Details
Get Picking Status
Get Receiving History
Get Material Movement History

Gemini reads the user's question and decides which tool should be called first.

For example, if the question is about a GR, Gemini may first call the Material Movement History tool.

Our backend receives this tool request and calls the corresponding REST API in either the Inventory microservice or the Material Movement microservice.

The API returns real data from the database.

We then send that response back to Gemini.

Gemini now has more information and decides whether it needs another tool.

For example, after checking material movement, it may decide to check Allocation Details, and then Picking Status.

This process continues until Gemini has enough information to answer the user's question.

We call this an agentic loop because the AI is deciding which tools to call step by step.

To avoid infinite loops, we limited it to a maximum of six tool rounds.

Final Response

Once Gemini has collected all the required information, it returns a structured response containing:

the final answer,
findings,
recommendations,
and a confidence level.

For example, it may say:

"GR 1234 is waiting because Allocation ALO-9999 has been created but no picker has been assigned."

So instead of checking multiple systems manually, the warehouse manager immediately gets the root cause.

Reliability

One important thing is that the AI never generates inventory information on its own.

It only uses data returned from our backend APIs.

So every answer is based on real database records, which prevents hallucinations.

Additional Features

We also store every tool call in Redis for 24 hours.

This includes:

which tools Gemini called,
the arguments,
response time,
and whether the call succeeded or failed.

This helps us debug investigations and monitor AI behavior.

Technologies Used

The project was built using:

NestJS
TypeScript
Google Gemini Function Calling
REST APIs
Redis
Axios with retry mechanism
Microservices Architecture

Overall, this feature reduced investigation time from around 30–60 minutes to just a few seconds, while allowing warehouse users to ask questions in natural language instead of navigating multiple screens."

