If they ask "What if Gemini has only 60 RPM?"

You can answer:

"If Gemini allows only 60 requests per minute and we receive 500 requests, I would not send all 500 immediately. First, I would apply rate limiting per user to block excessive usage. Then I would enqueue the remaining valid requests using Bull Queue and process them at a controlled rate. If the queue becomes too large or the provider continues returning 429 errors, I would temporarily reject new requests with a 429 or 503 response instead of continuously calling Gemini."

One more thing you can mention (this impresses interviewers)

"Another optimization is request validation before the AI call. If mandatory fields like warehouseCode or referenceNo are missing, or the question is empty, we immediately return a validation error instead of invoking Gemini. We can also cache answers for frequently asked questions using Redis. If the same investigation was already performed recently, we return the cached result without calling the LLM again. This further reduces cost, latency, and token usage."

This demonstrates that you're thinking about production systems, where protecting the LLM and controlling costs are just as important as building the AI workflow itself.

